Use FTConstants.h.pkg   // the whole tags (CS_DivEnd ...) and the style sheet links (CS_PaletteCssLink ...)
Use FTDashIcons.pkg     // DashIcon - the glyph IconLabel puts in front of a button's words

/*
    Package:    FTMarkupBuilders.pkg
    Purpose:    The shared builders for FlexTron markup - one place that decides what a clickable
                element looks like, instead of every dialog writing the span or div out by hand.

    WHY GLOBAL FUNCTIONS.
    They were object functions on cFTTileMarkup first, reached as ChipHtml(ghoTileMarkup, ..). That
    works from the two boards, which load after that package, and fails at COMPILE in the ten dialogs
    that load before it - 74 errors, "Function Not Found in Expression BARBUTTONHTML". Chasing the
    include order would have fixed the symptom; the cause is that these are pure string builders with
    no state to belong to. As globals they need no handle, they are available from the first Use in
    the program, and - the point that matters here - a wrong name is a BUILD error rather than the
    runtime "Invalid message" that an object function gives (see the error-98 crash of 2026-08-21).

    THE GATE MODEL, which is the same everywhere:
    enabled emits data-ServerOnClick; disabled emits NONE, so a dead control resolves to nothing
    rather than reaching a handler that has to ask whether it should have run.

    Only the TAG and the disabled-class convention ever differed between the callers - the boards and
    the findings list use <span> with an "off" modifier, the dialog bars use <div> with "dis" - so
    each wrapper passes its own and neither convention had to move.
*/

/* Global Function: FTHtmlEscape

 & < and " as entities - the escape every builder below applies to text it puts into markup. The
 same three replacements as rfHtmlEscape in cEncodingHelpers.pkg, kept here since 2026-09-23 so the
 builders need no DFRefactor package; a second Global function of the same NAME would be error 4390.
 > and ' are left as they are, exactly as before, so the markup is byte-identical.
*/
Function FTHtmlEscape Global String sText Returns String
    String s
    Move (Replaces("&", sText, "&amp;")) to s
    Move (Replaces("<", s, "&lt;")) to s
    Move (Replaces('"', s, "&quot;")) to s
    Function_Return s
End_Function

/* Global Function: FTPlainText

 The text of HTML without its tags, for the clipboard: a paragraph, a heading, a list item or a list ends
 a line, a list item starts with a dash, every other tag goes, the file's own line breaks and indents are
 layout and go too, and the named characters a text uses - &amp; &lt; &gt; &quot; &nbsp; - are put back.
 The About dialog's Copy uses it (cFTAboutDialog.pkg).
*/
Function FTPlainText Global String sHtml Returns String
    String sText
    Integer iLt iGt

    Move sHtml to sText
    // the file's own line breaks and indents are layout, not text - out before the real breaks go in
    Move (Replaces((Character(13)), sText, "")) to sText
    Move (Replaces((Character(10)), sText, " ")) to sText
    Move (Replaces("<li>", sText, "- ")) to sText
    Move (Replaces("</p>", sText, C_NewLine)) to sText
    Move (Replaces("</li>", sText, C_NewLine)) to sText
    Move (Replaces("</h3>", sText, C_NewLine)) to sText
    Move (Replaces("</ul>", sText, C_NewLine)) to sText
    Move (Pos("<", sText)) to iLt
    While (iLt > 0)
        Move (Pos(">", sText)) to iGt
        If (iGt < iLt) ;
            Move 0 to iGt
        If (iGt = 0) Begin
            Move (Left(sText, (iLt - 1))) to sText
            Move 0 to iLt
        End
        Else Begin
            Move (Left(sText, (iLt - 1)) + Mid(sText, (Length(sText)), (iGt + 1))) to sText
            Move (Pos("<", sText)) to iLt
        End
    Loop
    Move (Replaces("&amp;", sText, "&")) to sText
    Move (Replaces("&lt;", sText, "<")) to sText
    Move (Replaces("&gt;", sText, ">")) to sText
    Move (Replaces("&quot;", sText, '"')) to sText
    Move (Replaces("&nbsp;", sText, " ")) to sText
    // the runs of spaces the indents left, and a space at the start of a line
    While (Pos("  ", sText) > 0)
        Move (Replaces("  ", sText, " ")) to sText
    Loop
    Move (Replaces((C_NewLine + " "), sText, C_NewLine)) to sText
    Function_Return (Trim(sText))
End_Function

/* Global Functions: StyleSheetLink, ScriptLink

 The <link> for a style sheet and the <script> for a script file, by path - what a page's head, and the
 includes the framework writes into a host page (OnDefineScriptIncludes), are made of. A query string is part
 of the path: StyleSheetLink("RDCFlexTron/ftcontrols.css?v=" + CS_FTLibraryVersion).
*/
Function StyleSheetLink Global String sHref Returns String
    Function_Return ('<link rel="stylesheet" href="' + sHref + '">')
End_Function

Function ScriptLink Global String sSrc Returns String
    Function_Return ('<script src="' + sSrc + '"></script>')
End_Function

/* Global Function: CssLink

 The <link> for one stylesheet in AppHtml/CssStyle, by its name - StyleSheetLink with the folder and the .css
 filled in. A function rather than a Define per sheet: there are a dozen page-specific sheets now, and a dozen
 more CS_*CssLink constants would say nothing that the file name does not.

 ORDER IS THE CONTRACT. Emit it AFTER CS_PaletteCssLink so a page rule still wins a tie against
 palette/buttons/bars, exactly as the inline <style> block it replaced did, and anything the caller
 adds in its own <style> is later still and beats both.

     Move (CS_PaletteCssLink + CssLink("testcompile") + '<div ..>') to sHtml
*/
Function CssLink Global String sName Returns String
    Function_Return (StyleSheetLink("CssStyle/" + sName + ".css"))
End_Function

/* Global Function: ElemHtml

 The primitive. Attribute order is fixed here: class, data-ServerOnClick, data-OnClickParam, title -
 which is the order every hand-written site already used, so converting them was provably a no-op.
 A blank sParam or sTip omits the attribute entirely rather than emitting an empty one.
*/
Function ElemHtml Global String sTag String sCls String sClsOff String sId String sParam ;
                  String sLabel String sTip Boolean bEnabled Returns String
    String sOut

    If (bEnabled = False) Begin
        Move ('<' + sTag + ' class="' + sClsOff + '"') to sOut
        If (Trim(sTip) <> "") ;
            Move (sOut + ' title="' + sTip + '"') to sOut
        Function_Return (sOut + '>' + sLabel + '</' + sTag + '>')
    End

    Move ('<' + sTag + ' class="' + sCls + '" data-ServerOnClick="' + sId + '"') to sOut
    If (Trim(sParam) <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' data-OnClickParam="' + sParam + '"') to sOut
    If (Trim(sTip) <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' title="' + sTip + '"') to sOut
    Function_Return (sOut + '>' + sLabel + '</' + sTag + '>')
End_Function

/* Global Function: ClickableHtml

 An element that is ALWAYS clickable - a card action, a crumb step, a pill, a row that opens
 something. No gate: these carry data-ServerOnClick unconditionally, so there is no disabled class to
 name and passing one would invent a state the caller does not have.

 The class may be an expression rather than a literal - several sites build it from a row's kind -
 which is why it is a plain String here and not a fixed set.
*/
Function ClickableHtml Global String sTag String sCls String sId String sParam String sLabel ;
                       String sTip Returns String
    Function_Return (ElemHtml(sTag, sCls, sCls, sId, sParam, sLabel, sTip, True))
End_Function

/* Global Function: OffElemHtml

 The THIRD disabled convention, and the one the wrappers above did not cover. ChipHtml adds " off"
 to the class and ButtonHtml adds " dis"; this one SWAPS the class for a differently named twin -
 .mtbtn becomes .mtbtn-off, .tcbtn becomes .tcbtn-off - so the pairing lives in the class name
 rather than in a modifier.

 Pass the ENABLED name. The "-off" is appended here, which is the whole point: eleven sites used to
 write the shut name out by hand, and a shut twin whose name nobody could grep for is how the five
 of them drifted out of shape in the first place (see buttons.css).

 No sId and no sParam - a shut element carries neither, and offering them would invent a state the
 caller does not have.
*/
Function OffElemHtml Global String sTag String sCls String sLabel String sTip Returns String
    Function_Return (ElemHtml(sTag, sCls, (sCls + "-off"), "", "", sLabel, sTip, False))
End_Function

/* Global Function: ChipHtml

 A <span> chip - the boards, the findings list, the package cards. Disabled class is "<cls> off".
*/
Function ChipHtml Global String sCls String sClsOff String sId String sParam String sLabel ;
                  String sTip Boolean bEnabled Returns String
    Function_Return (ElemHtml("span", sCls, sClsOff, sId, sParam, sLabel, sTip, bEnabled))
End_Function

/* Global Function: BarButtonHtml

 A <div> button in a dialog bar - .rpbtn in the report bars, .sfbtn in the select bars, and the
 per-dialog classes (.tcbtn, .csbtn, .ml-btn ..) that buttons.css already styles as one family.
 Disabled class is "<cls> dis", which is the convention those bars use.

 The CSS for all of it has lived in buttons.css since the button sweep, so this is the markup half of
 a consolidation that was already half done.
*/
/* ============================================================================================
   THE ELEMENT BUILDERS

   670 source lines in this app build HTML by hand, and div + span are 80% of what they build.
   These four cover that surface. The clickable family below (ElemHtml / ChipHtml / ButtonHtml)
   still owns anything carrying a data-ServerOnClick; these are for the markup around it.

   TWO FLAVOURS, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS. TagHtml takes inner MARKUP and passes it through;
   TagText takes plain TEXT and escapes it. Choosing the wrong one either double-escapes a
   nested element or lets a path with an & break the page - which is exactly the decision every
   call site was making by hand, sometimes by forgetting. Escaping is FTHtmlEscape
   (top of this package), the same three replacements as cEncodingHelpers' rfHtmlEscape.

   NO CLOSING-TAG BUILDER, on purpose. A closing tag never varies, so it is a constant -
   CS_DivEnd, CS_SpanEnd and the rest, in FTConstants.h.pkg, which this package Uses.

   A BLANK sCls emits no class attribute, so these serve unclassed elements too.
   ============================================================================================ */
Function OpenHtml Global String sTag String sCls Returns String
    If (sCls = "") ;
        Function_Return ('<' + sTag + '>')
    Function_Return ('<' + sTag + ' class="' + sCls + '">')
End_Function

/* THE TWO THAT CALL SITES ACTUALLY USE, and they exist to READ as a pair with the closing
   constants (Nils 2026-08-25):

       Move (sHtml + (OpenHtml("div", "st")) + ... + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml     <- what it was
       Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("st"))         + ... + CS_DivEnd) to sHtml     <- what it is

   The old form put the tag in a STRING PARAMETER at one end of the pair and baked it into the
   NAME at the other, so the two halves did not look like halves - you had to notice that "div"
   and CS_DivEnd referred to the same thing. Now the word Div appears in both, the verbs pair,
   and the tag name is never typed at a call site at all.

   Every OpenHtml call in the app was one of these two. OpenHtml stays as the plumbing they are
   built from, and for any tag that ever needs it. */
/* THE SAME, CARRYING A TOOLTIP. Without these a call site builds the attribute itself:

       Move (' title="' + CS_DashTidyDoneTip + '"') to sReadyTip
       Move (sHtml + '<div class="st done rdy"' + sReadyTip + '>' + ...) to sHtml

   - which is HTML in two places at once, and leaves the opening tag SPLIT across an expression
   so no converter can see it whole. With these the variable holds the TEXT and the tag stays in
   one piece (Nils 2026-08-25: "why the '<div class...'?").

   The tip is escaped. A title attribute is the likeliest place for a stray quote to close the
   attribute early and swallow the rest of the tag, and the value is often composed at runtime. */
/* A CLICKABLE OPENING, for a container whose content is built up afterwards. ClickableHtml builds
   a WHOLE element - open, label, close - which suits a button; these suit a card or a row that is
   opened here and closed many lines later.

   The attribute order and the omit-when-blank rules are ElemHtml's exactly, so a hand-written tag
   converts to one of these without the bytes moving. Like ElemHtml, they do NOT escape the tip:
   several callers pass a value they have already escaped themselves. */
Function DivOpenClick Global String sCls String sId String sParam String sTip Returns String
    String sOut
    Move ('<div class="' + sCls + '" data-ServerOnClick="' + sId + '"') to sOut
    If (Trim(sParam) <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' data-OnClickParam="' + sParam + '"') to sOut
    If (Trim(sTip) <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' title="' + sTip + '"') to sOut
    Function_Return (sOut + '>')
End_Function

Function SpanOpenClick Global String sCls String sId String sParam String sTip Returns String
    String sOut
    Move ('<span class="' + sCls + '" data-ServerOnClick="' + sId + '"') to sOut
    If (Trim(sParam) <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' data-OnClickParam="' + sParam + '"') to sOut
    If (Trim(sTip) <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' title="' + sTip + '"') to sOut
    Function_Return (sOut + '>')
End_Function

/* A blank class DROPS the attribute, exactly as OpenHtml does - a tag carrying nothing but a
   tooltip is a real shape (nine of them in the Libraries dialog), and emitting class="" for it
   would change the bytes. */
Function DivOpenTip Global String sCls String sTip Returns String
    If (sCls = "") ;
        Function_Return ('<div title="' + (FTHtmlEscape(sTip)) + '">')
    Function_Return ('<div class="' + sCls + '" title="' + (FTHtmlEscape(sTip)) + '">')
End_Function

Function SpanOpenTip Global String sCls String sTip Returns String
    If (sCls = "") ;
        Function_Return ('<span title="' + (FTHtmlEscape(sTip)) + '">')
    Function_Return ('<span class="' + sCls + '" title="' + (FTHtmlEscape(sTip)) + '">')
End_Function

/* THE SAME PAIR, TAKING THE TIP AS MARKUP. Named -Html for the reason TagHtml is: in this file,
   Html means "already the thing" and Text means "will be escaped for you". Choosing between them
   is choosing who owns the escaping.

   USE THE ESCAPING ONES. DivOpenTip / SpanOpenTip are the default and always will be: a tooltip is
   the likeliest place for a stray quote to close the attribute early and swallow the rest of the
   tag, and most tips are composed at runtime from names, paths and counts.

   These two exist for the tips that are ALREADY correct HTML and would be spoilt by escaping:

     * a tip carrying a CHARACTER ENTITY. "&#10;" is how a title attribute gets a line break and
       "&rsquo;" is how it gets an apostrophe that will not close anything. Escape either and the
       & becomes &amp; and the reader sees the entity spelled out instead of the character.
     * a tip the caller has ALREADY escaped - HtmlEscaped / ScEscaped / Esc at the call site.
       Escaping twice turns & into &amp;amp;.

   If you are reaching for these for any other reason, you want the escaping pair. */
Function DivOpenTipHtml Global String sCls String sTipHtml Returns String
    If (sCls = "") ;
        Function_Return ('<div title="' + sTipHtml + '">')
    Function_Return ('<div class="' + sCls + '" title="' + sTipHtml + '">')
End_Function

Function SpanOpenTipHtml Global String sCls String sTipHtml Returns String
    If (sCls = "") ;
        Function_Return ('<span title="' + sTipHtml + '">')
    Function_Return ('<span class="' + sCls + '" title="' + sTipHtml + '">')
End_Function

/* AN OPENING TAG THAT KEEPS AN INLINE STYLE, and an <img> the same way.

   Nearly every style belongs in a .css file - that is what the CssStyle folder is for, and a class
   follows the theme where a hardcoded colour cannot. These two exist for the handful of places
   where the style has to apply at FIRST PAINT, before any stylesheet has been fetched:

     * a box whose child sizes itself against it. The Libraries badge is 32x32 from libraries.css,
       and for the frame before that sheet applies it is an auto-height div - so an <img> asking
       for height:100% has nothing to resolve against and paints at its INTRINSIC size, which for
       a multi-resolution .ico is up to 256px (Nils 2026-08-24, the flashing "VDF").
     * a value computed per element - a bar's height, a column's width. No class can carry it.

   If neither applies, use DivOpen and put the rule in the sheet. Passing a style here is a claim
   that a stylesheet arriving one frame late would be visibly wrong. */
Function DivOpenStyle Global String sCls String sStyle Returns String
    If (sCls = "") ;
        Function_Return ('<div style="' + sStyle + '">')
    Function_Return ('<div class="' + sCls + '" style="' + sStyle + '">')
End_Function

Function SpanOpenStyle Global String sCls String sStyle Returns String
    If (sCls = "") ;
        Function_Return ('<span style="' + sStyle + '">')
    Function_Return ('<span class="' + sCls + '" style="' + sStyle + '">')
End_Function

/* An image. sCls, sStyle and sOnError are all optional and all dropped when blank, so the plain
   form is ImgHtml(sSrc, "", "", ""). alt="" is always emitted: these are decorative, and an <img>
   with no alt at all is read aloud by a screen reader as its file name.

   sOnError takes the handler BODY, not the attribute - the quoting is done here, which is the
   whole point: a handler is JavaScript inside an HTML attribute inside a DataFlex literal, and
   that is three levels of quoting for a call site to get right on its own. */
Function ImgHtml Global String sSrc String sCls String sStyle String sOnError Returns String
    String sOut

    Move '<img' to sOut
    If (sCls <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' class="' + sCls + '"') to sOut
    Move (sOut + ' src="' + sSrc + '" alt=""') to sOut
    If (sStyle <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' style="' + sStyle + '"') to sOut
    If (sOnError <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' onerror="' + sOnError + '"') to sOut
    Function_Return (sOut + '>')
End_Function

Function DivOpen Global String sCls Returns String
    Function_Return (OpenHtml("div", sCls))
End_Function

Function SpanOpen Global String sCls Returns String
    Function_Return (OpenHtml("span", sCls))
End_Function

Function TagHtml Global String sTag String sCls String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return ((OpenHtml(sTag, sCls)) + sInner + '</' + sTag + '>')
End_Function

Function TagText Global String sTag String sCls String sText Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml(sTag, sCls, (FTHtmlEscape(sText))))
End_Function

/* The same, carrying a tooltip. Both the tip and the text are escaped - a title attribute is
   the likeliest place for a quote to close the attribute early and swallow the rest of the tag. */
Function TagTipText Global String sTag String sCls String sTip String sText Returns String
    Function_Return ('<' + sTag + ' class="' + sCls + '" title="' + (FTHtmlEscape(sTip)) ;
        + '">' + (FTHtmlEscape(sText)) + '</' + sTag + '>')
End_Function

/* ============================================================================================
   PROSE - the vocabulary a help panel is written in.

   These exist so a dialog can be written in DataFlex rather than in HTML. A developer who has
   never written a tag can read HelpHeading / HelpParagraph / CodeText and know exactly what the
   panel will look like; the tag names and class names stay in here, which is where someone who
   does know HTML would go looking anyway.

   CodeText EARNS ITS PLACE ON ESCAPING ALONE. The Add Package panel used to carry
   '&lt;publisher&gt;/&lt;package&gt;#&lt;version&gt;' in the source - correct, and unreadable unless you
   know HTML entities. It is now CodeText("<publisher>/<package>#<version>"), which produces the
   same bytes. Note it escapes > as well as < and &, which FTHtmlEscape does not: a bare > is legal
   in text, but code samples are exactly where a reader expects to see the angle brackets they
   typed come back unchanged.
   ============================================================================================ */
Function HelpHeading Global String sText Returns String
    Function_Return (TagText("h4", "", sText))
End_Function

// Takes MARKUP, because a paragraph is where BoldText and CodeText get composed into a sentence.
// A paragraph carrying a class; HelpParagraph is this with none. Both take MARKUP.
// Defined FIRST because HelpParagraph calls it - a global must exist before its caller compiles.
Function ParaHtml Global String sCls String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("p", sCls, sInner))
End_Function

Function HelpParagraph Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (ParaHtml("", sInner))
End_Function

Function BoldText Global String sText Returns String
    Function_Return (TagText("b", "", sText))
End_Function

/* Bold around MARKUP, the pair to BoldText exactly as HelpParagraph is the pair to it: Text is
   escaped for you, Html is already the thing.

   Most bold in this app wraps a count and a few words, and BoldText is right for those. This one
   is for a <b> whose content is BUILT - a literal, an interpolated value, another element - where
   escaping would either change bytes that are already correct or reach a runtime value whose
   escaping is the caller's business, not the builder's. */
/* AN EMPTY <i>. In this app the <i> tag is never italic text - it is a decorative SHAPE: a dot in
   a strip, a bar, the coloured part of a progress track. Every one of them is styled by a
   descendant rule (.sc-vid-dots i, .sc-vid-track i) and carries no content, so the whole element
   is its class or nothing at all.

   NOT called BarHtml: four dialogs already have their own Function BarHtml for their button bar,
   and a global of that name collides with every one of them - error 4390, the duplicate-global
   this very program refactors. */
Function ShapeHtml Global String sCls Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("i", sCls, ""))
End_Function
Function BoldHtml Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("b", "", sInner))
End_Function

/* ============================================================================================
   TABLES. Six names for the five tags a table is made of, so a row of data reads as a row of
   data instead of as punctuation. All of them take MARKUP - a cell holds whatever the caller
   built, commonly another builder - and none escapes; a cell whose text needs escaping should
   arrive escaped, the same rule the rest of the -Html family follows.

   The two Tip forms exist because a header cell explaining itself is the ordinary case, not a
   special one: five of the nine columns in the progress history carry a title.
   ============================================================================================ */
Function TableHtml Global String sCls String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("table", sCls, sInner))
End_Function

Function TheadHtml Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("thead", "", sInner))
End_Function

Function TbodyHtml Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("tbody", "", sInner))
End_Function

Function RowHtml Global String sCls String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("tr", sCls, sInner))
End_Function

Function CellHtml Global String sCls String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("td", sCls, sInner))
End_Function

Function HeadCellHtml Global String sCls String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("th", sCls, sInner))
End_Function

/* An element with a class, a tooltip and content, none of it escaped - TagTipText's -Html twin.
   A blank class drops the attribute, as everywhere else here. */
Function TagTipHtml Global String sTag String sCls String sTip String sInner Returns String
    String sOut

    Move ('<' + sTag) to sOut
    If (sCls <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' class="' + sCls + '"') to sOut
    If (sTip <> "") ;
        Move (sOut + ' title="' + sTip + '"') to sOut
    Function_Return (sOut + '>' + sInner + '</' + sTag + '>')
End_Function

// The two cell forms carrying a tooltip. A header cell explaining itself is the ordinary case:
// five of the nine columns in the progress history carry one.
Function CellTipHtml Global String sCls String sTip String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagTipHtml("td", sCls, sTip, sInner))
End_Function

Function HeadCellTipHtml Global String sCls String sTip String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagTipHtml("th", sCls, sTip, sInner))
End_Function

Function CodeText Global String sText Returns String
    String s
    Move (Replaces("&", sText, "&amp;")) to s
    Move (Replaces("<", s, "&lt;")) to s
    Move (Replaces(">", s, "&gt;")) to s
    Function_Return (TagHtml("code", "", s))
End_Function

/* Global Function: MarkedText

 Plain TEXT with its names marked the way a README marks them, between backticks: each one comes out in
 <code>, and the rest is escaped (FTHtmlEscape), so a < or an & in the text needs no entity.
 MarkedText("Set `piColumnSpan` to 10") gives Set <code>piColumnSpan</code> to 10.

 It lets a page's prose be a """ string - and a constant cannot go inside a string, so without it every
 name in the prose was a <code> tag written by hand:

     Append sItems (BulletItem(MarkedText("""
         This page is a `cFTHtmlPage`, built with `FTMarkupBuilders`.
         """)))

 It returns MARKUP: give it to TagHtml, BulletItem or HelpParagraph, never to TagText, which would escape
 the <code> it made. A backtick with no partner is left as it is.
*/
Function MarkedText Global String sText Returns String
    String sRest sAfter sOut
    Integer iOpen iClose

    Move (FTHtmlEscape(sText)) to sRest
    Move "" to sOut
    Move (Pos("`", sRest)) to iOpen
    While (iOpen > 0)
        Move (Right(sRest, (Length(sRest) - iOpen))) to sAfter
        Move (Pos("`", sAfter)) to iClose
        If (iClose > 0) Begin
            Move (sOut + Left(sRest, (iOpen - 1)) + CS_CodeOpen + Left(sAfter, (iClose - 1)) + CS_CodeEnd) to sOut
            Move (Right(sAfter, (Length(sAfter) - iClose))) to sRest
            Move (Pos("`", sRest)) to iOpen
        End
        Else ;
            Move 0 to iOpen
    Loop
    Function_Return (sOut + sRest)
End_Function

// A plain bulleted list. Items are markup, so an item may itself carry CodeText or a link.
Function BulletList Global String sItems Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("ul", "", sItems))
End_Function

Function BulletItem Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("li", "", sInner))
End_Function

/* Global Function: SepHtml

 The upright bar between two readings in a status strip. Four sites in three files spelled the
 span out; the glyph and its class live here now.
*/
Function SepHtml Global Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("span", "sep", "|"))
End_Function


/* ============================================================================================
   THE TILE vocabulary. A dashboard tile is a glyph, a heading and a line of explanation; the
   step strip above it is a numbered or ticked marker per stage. Both boards draw these from
   cFTTileMarkup, so the names are worth more here than in most places - the same call renders
   on two different pages.

   Content passes through unescaped, as with the findings vocabulary: TileIcon is handed the
   markup DashIcon returns, and the headings are literal English written right at the call.
   ============================================================================================ */
Function TileIcon Global String sGlyph Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("div", "dash-ico", sGlyph))
End_Function

Function TileHeading Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("div", "dash-h", sInner))
End_Function

Function TileParagraph Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("div", "dash-p", sInner))
End_Function

// The step strip's marker: a number while the stage is ahead of you, a tick once it is done.
Function StepMarker Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("span", "d", sInner))
End_Function

/* ============================================================================================
   THE FINDINGS ROW vocabulary. A row on the dashboard findings list is a dot, a badge, a file
   or name, a message and a hint; these name those parts so the board can be read without
   knowing which span carries which class.

   They take their content AS GIVEN and do not escape it. Every caller composes the inner value
   itself - a function name, a count, a path that one site already puts through HtmlEscaped -
   and escaping here would double-escape that site while silently changing the others. The
   escaping decision stays where the value comes from, which is the only place that knows.
   ============================================================================================ */
Function FindingBadge Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("span", "bdg bdg-c", sInner))
End_Function

Function FindingFile Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("span", "ffile", sInner))
End_Function

Function FindingMsg Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("span", "fmsg", sInner))
End_Function

Function FindingHint Global String sInner Returns String
    Function_Return (TagHtml("span", "fhint", sInner))
End_Function

/* Global Function: FindingRepOpen

 Opens a REPORT row on the findings list: the row div, its severity dot, and the opening tag of
 the badge beside it. Nine sites built this by hand, differing only in the dot class and in what
 went inside the badge.

 IT RETURNS AN OPEN TAG, deliberately. These call sites build their row by appending - the badge
 text is a count, a message number or a word, and some rows carry an <span class="fmsg"> after it
 and some do not. Folding the whole row would need a parameter per variant; folding the part that
 never varies is what puts the row SHAPE in one place, which is the thing that was duplicated.
 Close the badge span at the call site (CS_SpanEnd), as before.

 sDot is the severity class: "e" error, "w" warning, "s" scan.
*/
Function FindingRepOpen Global String sDot Returns String
    Function_Return ((DivOpen("fnd rep")) + (TagHtml("span", ("fdot " + sDot), "")) + (SpanOpen("bdg bdg-c")))
End_Function

/* Global Function: IconLabel

 The FACE of a button - its 15px glyph and the words beside it. The builders below own the
 ELEMENT (classes, id, click wiring, the disabled twin); this owns what goes inside it, which
 every call site used to write out by hand.

 It was spelled out at 72 sites across 16 files. That is 72 places to get the path convention
 wrong, and 72 edits the day a standard button changes its glyph - "Close" alone appeared nine
 times in eight files, "Select All" and "Invert" five times each in five.

 sIcon is a glyph NAME - DashIcon's, or one of the application's own (FTAppGlyphs.pkg):
 IconLabel(CS_GlyphClose, "Close") gives the glyph inline, in the button's text colour, so it follows
 the theme, then Close. A name with a dot is an image file under AppHtml instead,
 IconLabel("Images/Logo.png", "Logo"), drawn as it is. Until 0.9.0 sIcon was the stem of a file in
 AppHtml\Svg - an <img>, in the one colour it was drawn in. buttons.css sizes both inside a button
 of the family, so nothing here states a size.

 Faces built INTO a larger markup string are left alone - splitting a hand-built element to
 reach its icon buys nothing.
*/
Function IconLabel Global String sIcon String sLabel Returns String
    If (Pos(".", sIcon) > 0) ;
        Function_Return (ImgHtml(sIcon, "", "", "") + sLabel)
    Function_Return (DashIcon(sIcon) + sLabel)
End_Function

/* Global Function: ButtonHtml

 A button of the family buttons.css styles as one - .dfrbtn .rpbtn .sfbtn .csbtn .ml-btn .mph-btn
 .psbtn .tcbtn .mtbtn .sabtn .cw-btn .apb .act .cfb .tcbtnc. The disabled class is "<cls> dis"
 for every one of them; buttons.css lists exactly that set, which is what makes them a family
 rather than fifteen lookalikes.

 It also builds the ONE thing here that is not a button: the Add Package dialog's .excp "Copy
 link", a text link styled in addpackage.css (2026-08-24). What this function actually provides
 is the data-ServerOnClick wiring, and a link needs that as much as a button does.

 sCls may carry modifiers - "tcbtn tc-scan", "mph-btn mph-reset" - and "dis" is appended after them,
 which is the order the hand-written sites already used.

 The TAG is a parameter because the family is split between <div> and <span> for no reason anyone
 chose; passing it keeps every site rendering exactly what it rendered before.
*/
Function ButtonHtml Global String sTag String sCls String sId String sParam String sLabel ;
                    String sTip Boolean bEnabled Returns String
    Function_Return (ElemHtml(sTag, sCls, (sCls + " dis"), sId, sParam, sLabel, sTip, bEnabled))
End_Function

// The <div> half of the family - the dialog bars. Its 45 callers pre-date ButtonHtml.
Function BarButtonHtml Global String sCls String sId String sParam String sLabel ;
                       String sTip Boolean bEnabled Returns String
    Function_Return (ButtonHtml("div", sCls, sId, sParam, sLabel, sTip, bEnabled))
End_Function
